    1	# Free-Probability Moment Test on δ(x) — Results
    2	
    3	**Script:** `free_probability_delta.py`
    4	**Date:** 2026-04-26 (Session 53, fresh-perspective)
    5	**Verdict:** CLOSED — classical CLT (Gaussian) wins, no asymptotic freeness.
    6	**Failure mode:** **E** (equivalence — empirical distribution equates to a
    7	generic limit law from which no algorithmic structure is derivable).
    8	
    9	## Goal
    10	
    11	The 2026-04-26 morning brainstorm
    12	(`fresh_perspective_session_2026_04_26.md`, item 3) proposed: treat
    13	the contribution of each non-trivial zeta zero ρ to δ(x) at log-scale
    14	as a free random variable. The R-transform composes additively under
    15	free convolution, so if `δ(x)/√x` over a dyadic window matches a known
    16	free distribution (free Wigner / semicircle, free Poisson, Marchenko–
    17	Pastur), one could in principle invert via R-transform and bypass
    18	explicit zero summation.
    19	
    20	## What was computed
    21	
    22	For `n_max = 2¹⁸ = 262,144`, sieve `π(n)` and evaluate `Li(n)` on the
    23	integer grid (mpmath, ~9.5 s). Form the Selberg-scaled fluctuation
    24	
    25	  z_n = (π(n) − Li(n)) · log(n) / √n     — variance ~ O(1).
    26	
    27	Standardize z within each dyadic window `[2^j, 2^{j+1})`, compute
    28	sample moments `m_k = E[zₛ^k]` for `k = 1..6`, and compare against
    29	the standardized moments of four reference distributions.
